From 91e94c532c9c37c40d33f643ceab1f403aee067c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vasilii Zolotukhin <vasilii.zolotukhin@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 13:01:22 +1000
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] Mac: Plurium updates through Homebrew
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A minute after startup and then every six hours Plurium compares the
version in its published cask (Casks/plurium.rb in indapublic/plurium,
which is what `brew upgrade` installs) with the installed one, read from
the Homebrew Caskroom. A newer version shows an "Update" button at the
end of the unified tab strip and turns the "Check for Updates…" item
after "About Plurium" into "Update to <version>…". chrome://settings/help
uses the same check instead of Chromium's updater (one-line hook in
version_updater_mac.mm) and offers "Relaunch" for an available update.

Updating is a regular chrome::AttemptRelaunch(), so every profile's
session is restored and pages can still cancel it. While an update is
available the relaunch command is replaced
(upgrade_util::SetRelaunchChromeBrowserCallbackForTesting(), the only
hook the relaunch has): a detached script waits for the process to
exit, runs `brew update` and `brew upgrade --cask
indapublic/plurium/plurium`, and reopens the app with the relaunch
switches. The log is ~/Library/Logs/Plurium/update.log.

Copies not installed with Homebrew only point to the release page.
Automatic checks can be turned off with the PluriumAutomaticUpdateChecks
user default; --plurium-update-feed=<url> replaces the cask URL for
testing. The About page's update texts say Plurium.
